The only way to guarantee that the `while` loop will terminate is to make sure that the loop condition eventually becomes false.  
In the code you posted the loop condition is

```c
while (i < 10)
```

and the only statement that changes `i` is

```c
i++;
```

So the loop will terminate **iff** `i` is increased on every iteration.  
If you want to be absolutely sure that the loop will finish, you can add a safety guard:

```c
int i = 0;
int maxIterations = 1000;   // some large number
int guard = 0;

while (i < 10 && guard < maxIterations) {
    printf("%d\n", i);
    i++;
    guard++;                // guard against infinite loops
}
```

Now the loop will stop either when `i` reaches 10 or when the guard counter reaches `maxIterations`.  
In practice, if you only have `i++` inside the loop and no other code that could modify `i`, the loop will always terminate after 10 iterations.
